Vendored supabase-js (self-hosted, SRI-pinned)
==============================================

Why this exists
---------------
The Supabase JS client drives authentication (login, registration, session/JWT
handling). It used to load from the public jsDelivr CDN with a *floating* major
version (@2) and no Subresource Integrity. That is a supply-chain weakness for a
security-critical script: the browser would execute whatever bytes the CDN
returned, and a CDN outage broke login entirely.

This directory holds the bundle vendored at an exact pinned version, served from
CineBox's own origin and loaded with an SRI integrity hash (fail-closed). The CSP
`script-src` in server.py is correspondingly tightened to `'self'` (no jsDelivr).

Pinned version
--------------
@supabase/supabase-js  2.108.1

Vendored file
-------------
2.108.1/supabase.min.js

  Source of truth: the npm package's own UMD artifact `dist/umd/supabase.js`
  (already Terser-minified by the package build), downloaded from
  https://unpkg.com/@supabase/supabase-js@2.108.1/dist/umd/supabase.js
  It is stored here as `supabase.min.js` (the bytes ARE the production-minified
  UMD build that defines the global `supabase` symbol).

  NOTE: we deliberately do NOT vendor jsDelivr's `dist/umd/supabase.min.js`
  path. jsDelivr *re-minifies* the package on the fly (its own banner warns
  "Do NOT use SRI with dynamically generated files"), so those bytes are not a
  stable, reproducible artifact. The npm package file is the canonical, stable
  artifact and is what carries a published integrity hash.

Integrity (SRI) hash — used in index.html
-----------------------------------------
  sha384-EjUdIVmzWliPzdzhxZ9ZoO0etXLKWuUPUftAGxP6qH6Lm4oLwoLaJR0Ba4pIDiDL

Authenticity cross-check
------------------------
The vendored bytes' sha256 equals the integrity unpkg publishes for this exact
package file, proving the bytes are the authentic, untampered npm artifact:
  sha256-06f9DJ3ul5CtbqhErClWxIkVQv0g6WQeRsyn+fcOcag=

How it is loaded
----------------
index.html (in <head>, SYNCHRONOUS — load order unchanged, the global
`supabase` symbol must exist before boot.js + the 7 deferred modules):

  <script src="vendor/supabase-js/2.108.1/supabase.min.js"
          integrity="sha384-EjUdIVmzWliPzdzhxZ9ZoO0etXLKWuUPUftAGxP6qH6Lm4oLwoLaJR0Ba4pIDiDL"
          crossorigin="anonymous"></script>

There is NO CDN fallback <script>. If the SRI check fails the browser refuses to
execute the bundle (fail-closed) — by design.

Upgrade procedure (single coordinated change)
---------------------------------------------
Upgrading supabase-js is one atomic change — never bump the file without bumping
the hash in the same commit, or the browser will block the new bundle.

1. Pick the new exact version (e.g. 2.X.Y). Download the canonical npm UMD file:
     curl -sL \
       "https://unpkg.com/@supabase/supabase-js@2.X.Y/dist/umd/supabase.js" \
       -o vendor/supabase-js/2.X.Y/supabase.min.js
   (create the new versioned directory; the version in the path is cache-busting.)

2. Recompute the SRI hash from the exact bytes you just downloaded:
     openssl dgst -sha384 -binary \
       vendor/supabase-js/2.X.Y/supabase.min.js | openssl base64 -A
   (Python alternative:
     python -c "import hashlib,base64,sys;print('sha384-'+base64.b64encode(hashlib.sha384(open(sys.argv[1],'rb').read()).digest()).decode())" \
       vendor/supabase-js/2.X.Y/supabase.min.js )

3. In index.html, update BOTH the src path (new version directory) and the
   integrity="sha384-..." attribute together.

4. Update the pinned version + hash recorded in this README and in
   CineBox/CLAUDE.md.

5. Remove the old version directory once the new one is verified loading.

6. Verify in a browser: auth still works, no jsDelivr request, and an
   intentionally corrupted byte makes the browser block the script.
